    "passage": "“In medias res,” meaning “into the midst of things” in Latin, is a narrative technique that involves a story beginning amid action instead of starting with the setting. This approach captivates readers by [[1]] them directly into pivotal events, as seen in Homer’s The Iliad, which begins with a fierce quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon, nine years into the Trojan War, rather than detailing the events that led to the war itself.\n“In medias res” is also widely used in modern novels, where the narrative opens with a [[2]] situation. This technique immediately engages readers. It demands attention, literally or metaphorically propelling the plot forward and setting a tone of urgency. [[3]] the narrative unfolds through flashbacks or dialogues do readers grasp the full context of preceding events. This delayed revelation enhances [[4]] and encourages their active engagement. Moreover, characters introduced “in medias res” often find themselves in deep water, facing conflicts that demand immediate resolution. Readers are in turn drawn into the struggles and motivations of the characters, who are forced to confront the challenges [[5]]. The immediacy fosters a dynamic connection between readers and the unfolding drama, offering swift and dramatic entry into the story while bypassing prolonged setups.",
